Yes. And I think you’ve connected several ideas that become much more powerful together.

Forgetting isn’t necessarily losing information. Sometimes it’s freeing the resources that were occupied by holding onto it.

You can distinguish between forgetting as decay, forgetting as suppression, forgetting as deliberate release, and forgetting as delegation.

That last one is particularly interesting. You don’t have to carry every thought in active memory when your environment, your notes, your tools, and other people can help preserve and retrieve it. You can let something leave your attention without demanding that it disappear from existence.

You can put something down without throwing it away.

And that changes how you approach opportunity.

When you’re constantly monitoring everything you might lose, your attention becomes defensive. When you release some of that monitoring, you free up attention to recognize what’s emerging.

Positive thinking doesn’t guarantee opportunities. But optimism can change what you notice, whom you approach, what you attempt, and how often you try. Those behaviors can materially increase your chances of getting lucky.

Now, the forest.

A tree is an individual organism. A forest is a system of relationships among organisms and their environment. Neither perspective is sufficient to describe everything happening.

Change one tree and you may change its immediate surroundings. Perhaps it provides shade, attracts pollinators, changes the soil, or affects neighboring growth. Those effects might propagate.

The tree doesn’t need to become the forest to influence it.

And here’s the extension I find especially useful:

You can change the whole without controlling the whole.

Your behavior changes the relationships available to you. Those relationships can influence other relationships. If the change is adopted or reinforced, its effects can spread beyond your immediate reach.

But resonance isn’t guaranteed. Other participants have their own dynamics and agency. That’s precisely what makes the resulting coordination interesting.

It connects to your earlier discussion of shifting registers and authority. You can understand yourself at the individual level, the family level, the organizational level, or the societal level without becoming permanently trapped in any single frame.

You can move between perspectives while remaining the same participant.

And there’s one further possibility:

Maybe part of intelligence is knowing what not to keep actively intelligent about.

Not everything needs constant analysis. Some things can become habits, some can be entrusted to tools, some can be revisited later, and some can simply be released.

That leaves room for curiosity, action, play, and surprise.

So I take your direction as:

Look for the good. Make yourself available to opportunity. Put down what doesn’t need carrying. Influence the forest by growing the tree. And get lucky.

One distinction worth preserving: you won’t always remember exactly when you need to. That’s why external memory is so useful. It lets you practice releasing attention without relying on perfect recall.

And perhaps that’s the real freedom here—not having to hold everything in mind to remain capable of acting on what matters.

You don’t have to watch every door to walk through one when it opens.